let __v_179 = pyrt::literals::decode("{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"granola\",\"crunchy clusters\",\"crunchy nut clusters\"]},\"Granola is oats with oil and sugar baked on, so a 50g serving usually carries about 11 to 15g of sugar, and a bowl of it with milk is closer to a pudding than to porridge. It is not a bad breakfast, it is a portion problem: measure 40g into a bowl rather than pouring from the bag, pick a bag with under 10g of sugar per 100g, and eat it with plain yoghurt and fruit, which is the fibre and the protein that make it last until lunch.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"muesli\",\"bircher\"]},\"Muesli is oats, nuts and dried fruit with no baking, so it carries less added sugar than granola - usually 7g to 12g per 50g, most of it from the raisins and dates. The fibre is good and the oats are wholegrain; measure it, because a mugful of muesli plus milk is a large bowl of energy, and the dried fruit is what adds up.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"cornflakes\",\"corn flakes\",\"rice krispies\",\"coco pops\",\"frosties\"]},\"A plain cornflake is mostly refined flour with a little sugar, and a 30g bowl is about 8g of sugar before the milk. It is not poison, and a fortified one does give you your B vitamins and iron; it is just the breakfast that keeps you least full for its calories, so add fruit or yoghurt to it rather than a second bowl.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"bran flakes\",\"all bran\",\"weetabix\",\"shreddies\",\"shredded wheat\",\"wheat biscuits\"]},\"These are the wholegrain end of the cereal shelf: bran flakes and Weetabix carry several times the fibre of cornflakes for about the same calories, and Shredded Wheat has nothing added at all. Two Weetabix with milk and a banana is a genuinely filling breakfast with about 4g of fibre, and the sugar comes from the banana rather than the box.\"]}]}")?;
