let __v_228 = pyrt::literals::decode("{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"iron\",\"ferritin\"]},\"Iron carries oxygen round your body in red blood cells, which is why a shortage shows up as tiredness, breathlessness on the stairs, pallor and brittle nails or hair loss. Red meat, liver, lentils, beans, fortified cereal and dark green veg are the everyday sources, and vitamin C in the same meal helps you take it up. Too much iron is harmful, so tablets are for a low a blood test has found rather than for a guess.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"calcium\"]},\"Calcium is what bones and teeth are built from, and it also matters for muscles and for blood clotting: an adult needs about 700mg a day, which is a pint of milk or a pot of yoghurt and a matchbox of cheese. Vitamin D is what gets it into the bones, so the two go together. A supplement is worth it only if you cannot manage the dairy, and 500mg is plenty.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"vitamin d\",\"vit d\"]},\"Vitamin D is what lets your body take up calcium, so it is the bones and muscles that suffer without it, and a low is linked with tiredness and aching. Sun makes most of it, so in the UK take 10 micrograms (400 IU) a day from October to March, and all year if you cover up or are indoors a lot. More than 100 micrograms a day is too much.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"vitamin c\",\"vit c\"]},\"Vitamin C is for skin, gums, blood vessels and healing, and it can double how much iron you take up from a meal. 40mg a day is plenty, and one orange, a couple of peppers or a handful of broccoli covers it, so a supplement is money for nothing. It is destroyed by heat, so steam rather than boil where you can.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"vitamin b12\",\"b12\",\"b-12\"]},\"B12 is for nerves and red blood cells, and a shortage creeps up over years because the liver stores it: tingling hands, tiredness, a sore tongue and low mood are the usual signs. It is only in animal foods - meat, fish, milk, eggs - so if you eat vegan take it from fortified milk or cereal, or a tablet of about 10 micrograms a day.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"magnesium\"]},\"Magnesium is used in hundreds of reactions, from muscles and nerves to blood sugar, and an adult needs about 300mg a day. Nuts, seeds, wholemeal bread, porridge, beans, lentils and dark chocolate cover it, so a supplement buys little unless a blood test has found a low.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"zinc\"]},\"Zinc is for healing, immunity and the sense of taste and smell, and adults need about 9.5mg a day, 7mg for women: meat, shellfish, eggs, milk, beans and wholemeal bread cover it. More than 25mg a day for months works against copper, so a big zinc supplement is not worth it.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"potassium\"]},\"Potassium is what keeps the heart beating evenly and the muscles working, and adults want about 3,500mg a day. Potatoes, beans, lentils, milk, bananas and tomatoes are the everyday sources, and most people fall short of it without trying. If a kidney problem has been diagnosed, ask before adding potassium-rich food.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"folate\",\"folic acid\"]},\"Folate is for making new cells, which is why it matters most in early pregnancy: 400 micrograms a day from before conception until week 12 cuts the risk of spina bifida. Outside pregnancy, green veg, beans, lentils and fortified cereal cover the 200 micrograms an adult needs.\"]}]}")?;
