let __v_1805 = pyrt::literals::decode("{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"rice-healthy\",{\"$tuple\":[\"rice\"]},\"Rice on its own is about 130 calories per 100g cooked and not fattening: what goes with it and how much of it decides that. A mug of cooked rice, about 150g, is a normal portion, and brown rice has around three times the fibre of white.\",{\"$tuple\":[]}]},{\"$tuple\":[\"bread-healthy\",{\"$tuple\":[\"bread\",\"toast\",\"sourdough\",\"wrap\",\"wraps\",\"pitta\",\"bagel\"]},\"Bread is fine: two slices of wholegrain or seeded is about 6g of fibre and a fair base for a meal. The white sliced loaf has less fibre and less staying power, so keep it for toast and make the everyday one wholegrain.\",{\"$tuple\":[\"burnt\",\"burned\",\"charred\",\"scorched\",\"burnt toast\",\"burned toast\",\"black\"]}]},{\"$tuple\":[\"lentils-healthy\",{\"$tuple\":[\"lentils\",\"lentil\",\"beans\",\"chickpeas\",\"pulses\",\"hummus\"]},\"Lentils and beans are one of the best buys in the shop: a drained tin has around 8g of protein and 6g of fibre for about 120 calories. They count towards your five a day and they keep you full far longer than the same weight of rice.\",{\"$tuple\":[]}]},{\"$tuple\":[\"oily-fish-healthy\",{\"$tuple\":[\"salmon\",\"mackerel\",\"sardines\",\"tuna\",\"fish\",\"kippers\",\"trout\"]},\"Oily fish is worth two portions a week: the omega-3 in salmon, mackerel and sardines is what the heart and the joints use, and a 140g cooked portion is a serving. Tinned sardines and mackerel count and are the cheapest way to do it; tinned tuna is the lean one, so it gives protein rather than omega-3.\",{\"$tuple\":[\"feed fish\",\"feeds fish\",\"feeding fish\",\"fed fish\",\"feed a fish\",\"feed the fish\",\"feed my fish\",\"fish food\",\"goldfish\",\"aquarium\",\"fish tank\",\"tropical fish\",\"koi carp\",\"can i give my cat\",\"can i give my dog\",\"give my cat\",\"give my dog\",\"can my cat eat\",\"can my dog eat\",\"for my cat\",\"for my dog\"]}]},{\"$tuple\":[\"shellfish-healthy\",{\"$tuple\":[\"prawn\",\"prawns\",\"king prawns\",\"scallop\",\"scallops\",\"mussel\",\"mussels\",\"crab\",\"squid\",\"calamari\",\"oyster\",\"oysters\",\"shellfish\",\"prawn cocktail\"]},\"Shellfish is the leanest protein in the shop: 100g of prawns is about 100 calories and 20g of protein with barely any fat, and scallops and mussels are much the same, with iron and zinc in the molluscs. Prawns carry cholesterol, but that matters far less than the saturated fat in a diet, so it is the mayonnaise, the butter or the breadcrumbs that make a prawn dish heavy rather than the prawns.\",{\"$tuple\":[\"crab apple\",\"crab apples\",\"went off\",\"gone off\",\"smells off\",\"smells\",\"smell\",\"shell\",\"shells\",\"picked\",\"picking\",\"allergy\",\"allergic\",\"pregnant\",\"pregnancy\",\"open\",\"opened\",\"opening\",\"unopened\",\"shut\",\"closed\",\"dead\",\"didnt\",\"didn't\",\"stay shut\",\"stays shut\",\"stayed shut\"]}]},{\"$tuple\":[\"chicken-healthy\",{\"$tuple\":[\"chicken\",\"turkey\",\"chicken breast\",\"chicken thighs\"]},\"Chicken is lean protein - about 30g in a breast for 160 calories - so it is what you cook it in that matters. Skin off and not fried, it is one of the better everyday meats; the breaded and the deep-fried versions carry the fat.\",{\"$tuple\":[]}]},{\"$tuple\":[\"yoghurt-healthy\",{\"$tuple\":[\"yoghurt\",\"yogurt\",\"greek yoghurt\",\"fromage frais\",\"skyr\"]},\"Plain yoghurt is a good buy: about 10g of protein and a third of a day's calcium in a 150g pot. The fruit ones and the children's tubes are the ones with 12g of sugar or more, so buy plain and add your own fruit or a spoon of jam.\",{\"$tuple\":[]}]},{\"$tuple\":[\"eggs-healthy\",{\"$tuple\":[\"omelette\",\"omelettes\",\"frittata\",\"scrambled egg\",\"scrambled eggs\",\"boiled egg\",\"boiled eggs\",\"poached egg\",\"poached eggs\",\"fried egg\",\"fried eggs\"]},\"An egg is one of the cheapest good foods there is: about 78 calories and 6g of protein, with the B12 and vitamin D that people buy supplements for. The cholesterol in food turned out to matter far less than the saturated fat in the diet, so one or two a day is fine for most people. What makes an omelette heavy is the butter and the cheese, not the eggs.\",{\"$tuple\":[\"crack\",\"cracked\",\"shell\",\"shells\",\"rubbery\",\"fluffy\",\"filling\",\"fold\",\"cholesterol\",\"how many\",\"a day\",\"bad for you\",\"bad for me\"]}]},{\"$tuple\":[\"greens-healthy\",{\"$tuple\":[\"spinach\",\"kale\",\"chard\",\"leafy greens\",\"watercress\",\"spring greens\",\"cavolo nero\",\"swiss chard\"]},\"Spinach, kale and the other leafy greens are among the best value in the shop at about 25 to 35 calories per 100g: a bag wilted in a pan is one of your five a day and brings folate, vitamin C and vitamin K, plus nitrates that help blood pressure. The iron in spinach is real (2.7mg per 100g) but poorly absorbed, so eat it with something containing vitamin C - lemon, peppers, a glass of juice. One caution: if you get kidney stones, spinach is the leaf to go easy on, because of its oxalate.\",{\"$tuple\":[\"smoothie\",\"salad\",\"soup\",\"slug\",\"slugs\",\"limp\",\"wilted\",\"bag of\",\"dressing\"]}]},{\"$tuple\":[\"broccoli-healthy\",{\"$tuple\":[\"broccoli\",\"cauliflower\",\"sprouts\",\"brussels sprouts\",\"cabbage\",\"romanesco\",\"cavolo nero\"]},\"The brassicas are worth eating often and cost about 30 to 40 calories per 100g: broccoli has more vitamin C than an orange per 100g, and the whole family brings folate, vitamin K and fibre. Steam it for 4 or 5 minutes rather than boiling it to bits, since long cooking is what loses the vitamin C. There is nothing in them that detoxes anything - the reason to eat them is that they fill half a plate for very few calories.\",{\"$tuple\":[\"smoothie\",\"salad\",\"soup\",\"slug\",\"slugs\",\"patch\",\"are sprouting\",\"is sprouting\",\"have sprouted\",\"has sprouted\"]}]},{\"$tuple\":[\"carrots-healthy\",{\"$tuple\":[\"carrot\",\"carrots\"]},\"Carrots are about 40 calories per 100g and one decent carrot gives you more than a day's vitamin A, as beta-carotene. Cooked with a little fat - butter, oil, in a stew - you absorb more of it than from raw sticks, so both are worth eating. They do not turn you orange unless you eat them by the bagful for weeks, and they are one of the cheapest vegetables there is.\",{\"$tuple\":[\"smoothie\",\"soup\",\"cake\",\"muffin\",\"muffins\",\"juice\",\"slug\",\"slugs\",\"fly\",\"flies\"]}]},{\"$tuple\":[\"banana-healthy\",{\"$tuple\":[\"banana\",\"bananas\",\"apple\",\"apples\",\"orange\",\"oranges\",\"pear\",\"pears\"]},\"A banana is about 100 calories with 3g of fibre and around 400mg of potassium, and the sugar in it (about 14g) comes with that fibre and water, so it is a snack rather than a problem - fresh fruit is not what the 30g free-sugar limit is aimed at. A greener one has more resistant starch, which is good for the gut; a spotted one is sweeter and easier to digest before a run. One or two a day is fine, and the same holds for apples, oranges and pears at 60 to 80 calories each.\",{\"$tuple\":[\"smoothie\",\"loaf\",\"cake\",\"bread\",\"banana bread\",\"muffin\",\"muffins\",\"ice cream\",\"frozen\",\"ripen\",\"ripe\",\"green\",\"peel\",\"slug\",\"fly\",\"flies\",\"juice\",\"juices\",\"cider\",\"smoothies\",\"dried\",\"crisps\",\"chips\",\"tree\",\"trees\",\"grow\"]}]},{\"$tuple\":[\"avocado-healthy\",{\"$tuple\":[\"avocado\",\"avocados\",\"guacamole\"]},\"Avocado is a good food that is easy to overdo: half a medium one is about 150 calories and 15g of fat, nearly all of it monounsaturated, which is the kind that helps cholesterol rather than harming it. It also brings about 5g of fibre and more potassium than a banana per 100g. Half a day is a sensible portion; a whole one on toast is a meal, not a spread.\",{\"$tuple\":[\"ripen\",\"ripe\",\"brown\",\"black\",\"stone\",\"keep\",\"freeze\",\"toast\",\"smash\",\"guacamole\"]}]},{\"$tuple\":[\"gluten-healthy\",{\"$tuple\":[\"gluten\",\"seitan\",\"vital wheat gluten\"]},\"Gluten is just the protein in wheat, barley and rye, and it is only a problem for the roughly one in a hundred people with coeliac disease, or for a wheat allergy - for everyone else there is nothing to avoid. If bread and pasta leave you bloated, windy or tired, ask the GP for a coeliac blood test before you cut gluten out, because the test only works while you are still eating it. Wholegrain wheat is one of the best sources of fibre in an ordinary diet, so it is worth keeping unless a test says otherwise.\",{\"$tuple\":[\"free\",\"flour\",\"bread\",\"recipe\",\"bake\",\"baking\",\"cake\",\"coeliac\",\"celiac\",\"allergy\",\"allergic\",\"intolerant\",\"intolerance\",\"sensitivity\",\"cant eat\",\"can't eat\",\"cannot eat\",\"without\",\"avoid\",\"cut out\",\"cutting out\",\"for lunch\",\"for breakfast\",\"for dinner\",\"for tea\",\"for supper\",\"for pudding\",\"what can i have\",\"what can i eat\",\"lunch\",\"breakfast\",\"dinner\"]}]},{\"$tuple\":[\"nuts-healthy\",{\"$tuple\":[\"nuts\",\"almonds\",\"walnuts\",\"cashews\",\"hazelnuts\",\"pistachios\",\"pecans\",\"brazil nuts\",\"mixed nuts\"]},\"A 30g handful of nuts is about 175 calories and one of the better snacks you can buy: 6g of protein, 3g of fibre and fat that is mostly unsaturated, and people who eat them regularly tend to weigh less, not more, because they are satisfying. Walnuts are the one with omega-3 (plant ALA). Buy them unsalted and unsweetened, and count the handful out rather than eating from the bag.\",{\"$tuple\":[\"squirrel\",\"birds\",\"bird\",\"feed the\",\"nut free\",\"nut-free\",\"recipe\",\"cake\",\"roast\",\"roasted\",\"toast\",\"cost\"]}]},{\"$tuple\":[\"peanut-butter-healthy\",{\"$tuple\":[\"peanut butter\",\"peanut\",\"peanuts\",\"almond butter\"]},\"Peanut butter is a decent everyday food: two tablespoons is about 190 calories with 7g of protein and 2g of fibre, and it keeps you full. The catch is the jar - buy one whose only ingredients are peanuts and salt, since the sweetened ones add sugar and palm oil - and use a level tablespoon rather than a spoon over the sink.\",{\"$tuple\":[\"jar\",\"recipe\",\"cookie\",\"cookies\",\"biscuit\",\"cake\",\"smell\",\"rancid\",\"stir\"]}]},{\"$tuple\":[\"berries-healthy\",{\"$tuple\":[\"berries\",\"blueberries\",\"strawberries\",\"raspberries\",\"blackberries\",\"cherries\",\"grapes\"]},\"Berries are the best fruit for the calories - about 30 to 60 per 100g - and a 80g portion counts towards your five a day, with vitamin C, fibre and the red and purple pigments that give them their colour. Frozen ones are just as good and a lot cheaper out of season. A punnet is two or three portions, so tip half into a bowl rather than eating from the box.\",{\"$tuple\":[\"smoothie\",\"jam\",\"crumble\",\"pie\",\"cake\",\"muffin\",\"muffins\",\"freeze\",\"frozen\",\"ripen\",\"ripe\",\"keep\",\"wine\",\"slug\",\"slugs\",\"birds\",\"net\"]}]},{\"$tuple\":[\"dark-chocolate-healthy\",{\"$tuple\":[\"dark chocolate\",\"chocolate\",\"cocoa\",\"milk chocolate\"]},\"Dark chocolate is the one to keep in the house if you want chocolate: 70 per cent cocoa is about 600 calories per 100g, so two squares is 120 or so, and it carries iron and magnesium with less sugar than milk chocolate. The heart-health claims come from cocoa flavanols and need far more than a square a day, so treat it as a nicer 120 calories, not a supplement.\",{\"$tuple\":[\"recipe\",\"cake\",\"brownie\",\"brownies\",\"melt\",\"melted\",\"temper\",\"mould\",\"mold\",\"bloom\",\"gift\",\"easter\",\"hot chocolate\",\"store\",\"keep\",\"biscuit\",\"biscuits\"]}]},{\"$tuple\":[\"butter-healthy\",{\"$tuple\":[\"butter\",\"ghee\",\"lard\",\"double cream\"]},\"Butter is fine in small amounts and is not a health food: about 700 calories per 100g and half of it saturated fat, so a teaspoon on vegetables is 35 calories and a thick spread on two slices is nearer 200. The guideline is 20g of saturated fat a day for a woman and 30g for a man, which is why oil is the better everyday cooking fat and butter is the one for the taste.\",{\"$tuple\":[\"margarine\",\"spread\",\"than\",\"instead\",\"cook\",\"cooking\",\"fry\",\"frying\",\"bake\",\"baking\",\"soften\",\"melt\",\"melted\",\"goes off\",\"rancid\",\"churn\",\"beans\",\"peanut butter\",\"peanut\",\"peanuts\",\"nut butter\",\"almond butter\",\"cocoa butter\",\"shea butter\",\"bread and butter\",\"butterfly\",\"buttermilk\"]}]},{\"$tuple\":[\"mushrooms-healthy\",{\"$tuple\":[\"mushrooms\",\"mushroom\",\"chestnut mushrooms\",\"portobello\",\"shiitake\"]},\"Mushrooms are about 20 calories per 100g, so a whole punnet is a portion for under 50: they bring 3g of protein, B vitamins including riboflavin and niacin, and selenium, plus the savoury taste that makes them useful in a meal that is meant to fill you up. The vitamin D some packs advertise comes from being treated with UV light - it is real, but a few minutes in the sun does the same job.\",{\"$tuple\":[\"forage\",\"foraging\",\"pick\",\"picking\",\"poisonous\",\"grow\",\"growing\",\"kit\",\"slug\",\"slugs\",\"fly\",\"flies\",\"slimy\",\"recipe\",\"soup\",\"stuffed\",\"stew\",\"gravy\"]}]}]}")?;
